<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kocel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 876</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Koceľ</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kocel was a Slavic prince of Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pribina">Pribina</a>, who ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zalavar">Mosapurc</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-balaton">Lake Balaton</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the East Frankish kings from 861 to about 876. He welcomed the Byzantine missionaries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius">Cyril and Methodius</a> in 867 and supported their Slavonic liturgy, and at his request the pope made Methodius archbishop of Pannonia in 870. After his death the region returned to direct Frankish rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kocel.jpg" alt="Kocel" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kmiki87; derivative work by Bostjan46 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pannonian_Slavic_Duchy-cro.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
